NETWORKING & SECURITY CONFIGURATION
===================================

1. IP ADDRESS
-------------

An IP address identifies a device or server on a network.

Example:
192.168.1.10

In this project, the AWS EC2 server was accessed using its public IP address.


2. PUBLIC IP
------------

A public IP address allows a cloud server to communicate with the Internet.

The public IP of the EC2 instance was used to access the Nginx web page from a browser.


3. PRIVATE IP
-------------

A private IP is used for communication within a private network such as a VPC.

AWS resources can communicate with each other using private IP addresses.


4. PORTS
--------

A port identifies a specific network service running on a server.

Important ports used:

Port 22 - SSH
Used for secure remote access to the Linux server.

Port 80 - HTTP
Used to access the Nginx web server.


5. SSH
-------

SSH stands for Secure Shell.

It is used to securely connect to and manage a remote Linux server.

Example:

ssh -i key.pem user@PUBLIC-IP


6. DNS
------

DNS stands for Domain Name System.

DNS translates human-readable domain names into IP addresses.

Example:

example.com → IP address


7. FIREWALL
-----------

A firewall controls incoming and outgoing network traffic.

It helps protect servers from unauthorized network access.


8. AWS SECURITY GROUP
---------------------

An AWS Security Group acts as a virtual firewall for AWS resources such as EC2 instances.

The Security Group was configured with the required inbound rules.

Inbound rules:

SSH
Port: 22
Purpose: Secure server access

HTTP
Port: 80
Purpose: Access the Nginx web server


9. NETWORK FLOW
---------------

Internet
   |
   | HTTP - Port 80
   ↓
AWS Security Group
   |
   ↓
EC2 Linux Server
   |
   ↓
Nginx
   |
   ↓
Custom Web Page


